the great wonder of the elder scrolls  online is that it's fantastical sights  and sounds can inspire great  anticipation such vast landscapes must  Harbor grand adventures to go on and  epic tales to hear unfortunately it too  often turns out that these are simply  illusions of grandeur and not the real  deal it's single player fantasy  experience does not provide the emergent  adventuring for which the Elder Scrolls  series is known and it's massively  multiplayer side is diminished by the  game's tendency to punish you for  playing with others the Elder Scrolls  online more or less captures the fantasy  vibe you'd want from an Elder Scrolls  game at least the character creation  offers plenty of customization options  including four classes and a number of  familiar Elder Scrolls races and once  you leave the introductory dungeon the  possibilities are exciting the game's  sparse heads-up display and fully voiced  conversations pull you into the world  and the quests wisely avoid the kill 20  goblin cliches that plagued the genre  unfortunately in leaving behind the  usual MMOG questing cliches and focusing  on lengthy conversations with NPCs the  Elder Scrolls online creates different  kinds of problems as you move from one  place to the next you hear the same few  actors over and over again that may not  have been such a sin if their voices  weren't so distinct and recognizable and  if the game's writing focused on  developing characters rather than  pouring lore into your head you could  levy the same criticism against previous  Elder Scrolls games but where the single  player games focused on letting you  craft your own story this one doesn't  offer the same kind of freedoms and so  you move through Tamriel in the  prescribed direction trudging through  one long-winded tale after another  instead of conjuring one to call your  own luckily many of these tales are  intriguing ones during my travels I  stumbled upon a village with a terrible  secret and had to decide whether to free  its residents from their curse or leave  them haunted by the truth my favorite  moments for those in which I saw a story  come to life rather than  having it read to me from a script sadly  grouping with others hurts the games  attempts to immerse you when it's  stories you frequently make choices that  are then reflected in the world around  you thanks to the game's phasing  technology this is a neat touch until  you join other players you and a buddy  might enter a region only to have your  teammate turn invisible because he has  made a different choice and thus has  entered another instance in these ways  the game actively discourages you from  grouping with others this isn't to say  that the game doesn't provide some  opportunities for players to come  together with 4 player dungeons leading  the way it's easy to find a group and  get into a dungeon once you've reached  the appropriate level the action is fine  but it never crackles in part due to the  lifeless animations Elder Scrolls combat  has always been somewhat messy but it's  real-time nature usually communicates a  sense of played against flesh the Elder  Scrolls online combines old-fashioned  hotkey combat with the action-oriented  swordplay too mixed results you target  using an on-screen reticle and combat  timing is confined mainly by your mana  and stamina bars not by skill cooldowns  you can also block attacks and tumble  but this is not true action combat you  aren't limited to any given type of  weapon or armor however no matter which  class you choose and weapon types have  various skills associated with them  there's a good deal of freedom in how  you spend skill points which you spend  not just in combat skills but in  crafting skills as well the Elder  Scrolls online is at its best in its  player vs. player campaign here the  game's three factions fight for control  of Cyrodiil when the action heats up  battles are a lot of fun whether you and  your comrades are setting up a line of  defensive ballistas at the top of it  keeps walls or going for broke and  charging a nearby farm protected by NPCs  getting to a battle like this requires a  bit of patience however due to Serie  Dill's vast expanses and quick travel  limitations the Elder Scrolls Online's  technical troubles such as its array of  broken quests can be patched it's less  certain however that the single-player  and multiplayer sides of this  fantastical coin will ever complement  each other  that's too bad because when the stars  aligned the game seems poised for  greatness  hopefully the Elder Scrolls online will  one day get out of its own way souls of  the hard weapons
 

